WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.800 --> 00:00:10.067
In this walkthrough we'll see how
straight-through processing is configured,
and how to check what happened, in the OpenTRMS Workbench.

2
00:00:10.067 --> 00:00:21.033
Straight-through processing is what lets a
trade settle without anyone touching it. The
STP Rules Manager decides which trades qualify.

3
00:00:21.033 --> 00:00:35.100
Rules are evaluated in order, and the order column is the
whole point. The first rule that matches a trade decides its
route, so what sits at the top matters more than what sits below.

4
00:00:35.100 --> 00:00:49.133
Rules are reordered by dragging the handle. There is a
keyboard route as well — focus a handle, press Space to pick
it up, then use the arrow keys — so this is usable without a mouse.

5
00:00:49.133 --> 00:01:03.400
Expanding a rule shows the thresholds it applies —
product, currency, maximum notional, minimum rating and
maximum tenor. A trade must satisfy all of them to qualify.

6
00:01:03.400 --> 00:01:13.267
When you need to know what actually happened
to something, Audit Event History has it.
Choose a deal and it loads that deal's event stream.

7
00:01:13.267 --> 00:01:28.133
Every change is recorded in order, with a timestamp, the type of
event, a version number, and who caused it. The record is
append-only, so it shows what happened rather than what things look like now.

8
00:01:28.133 --> 00:01:41.367
And System Status is the first place to look when the
Workbench itself seems unwell. Overall health, when
end of day last ran, and whether each component is active.

9
00:01:41.367 --> 00:01:51.733
That completes Automation, Audit and Health. Every
surface shown here can also be opened from the agent
panel on the right, by asking for it in plain English.
